To solve this, we first condense the logarithms on the left-hand side.
\begin{align*} \log_{10} \left( (k - 2)! (k - 1)! \right) + 2 = 2 \log_{10} k! \\ \log_{10} \left( (k - 2)! (k - 1)! \right) = 2 \log_{10} k! - 2. \end{align*}

On the right-hand side, we can invoke the normal logarithm properties.
\begin{align*} \log_{10} \left( (k - 2)! (k - 1)! \right) = \log_{10} \left( k!^2 \right) - 2. \end{align*}

By exponentiation,
\begin{align*} (k - 2)! (k - 1)! = k!^2 \cdot 0.01. \end{align*}

Simplifying further, we have
\begin{align*} \left( \frac{k!}{(k - 2)!} \right)^2 = 25 \implies \left( \frac{k!}{(k - 2)! (k - 1) (k - 2)} \right)^2 = 25 \\ \frac{1}{(k - 1)} = \frac{5}{k} \implies k = 5(k - 1) \implies 4k = 5 \implies k = \boxed{5}. \end{align*}

